.claude/plans/PLAN-198/repass-rc1/claude-rc1-19.verdict.txt:30:- How it worked before. check_agent_spawn records each named spawn's CAN-edit path hashes as `spawn_file_assignment_recorded` (file_assignment.py:542-560). The write goes emit_generic → _dispatch_generic → _write_event → spool_append into the per-PID spool. That is the production default, because only tests pin CEO_AUDIT_SYNC_MODE=1 (_lib/testing.py:167). The next spawn's PreToolUse looks for overlaps only in the tail of the canonical audit-log.jsonl, within a 10-minute window. The opportunistic drain after an append (_audit_emit_parts/writer.py:589-591) almost never fires in a hook: should_drain() (prepare.py:83-122) runs right after the append has refreshed the spool's mtime, so the age trigger never trips and the count trigger needs 100 lines. reconcile_journal_at_session_start has no production caller. In v1.4.3-rc.1, the only thing that made the record visible to the next spawn was the forced exit drain at the end of the check_agent_spawn process.
.claude/plans/PLAN-198/repass-rc1/claude-rc1-19.verdict.txt:41:- So the cost of one drain is proportional to the whole backlog, while progress is 100 entries per drain. A non-hook process that imports audit_emit pays that cost at exit.
