.claude/plans/PLAN-198/repass-rc1/claude-rc1-25.log:61:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SM4WvaHwyqSPskHMtZ7XL4","type":"tool_result","content":"255\t\n256\t- `scan()` in `_lib/secret_patterns.py` matched the NFKC-normalized view\n257\t  of a text but reported offsets in that view, while `redact()` and\n258\t  `scan_and_redact()` cut the original text with them. A character whose\n259\t  NFKC form has another length (an ellipsis, a ligature) before a secret\n260\t  shifted the cut, so part or all of the secret could survive next to its\n261\t  label. The Codex and Grok egress redactor (`_lib/codex_egress_redact.py`)\n262\t  uses these functions. Every release since v1.0.0 has the defect. The\n263\t  signed v1.4.0 verdict declared this defect as its condition 58.\n264\t- `scan()` now reports offsets in the text passed in. It matches that text\n265\t  and its NFKC view, maps the view's matches back, and merges overlapping\n266\t  findings into one span. The output is the original text with only those\n267\t  spans replaced; no normalized character reaches it.\n268\t- The pattern catalog is unchanged.\n269\t- The fix is a commit the maintainer signed, after review by Codex and two\n270\t  Claude refuters over several rounds.\n271\t  Codex approved it with one condition, which predates the fix and is\n272\t  declared in Known-open. No sentinel file covers it.\n273\t\n274\t### Fixed — backup and restore\n275\t\n276\t- `ceo-backup.sh` archives only the regular files of `memory/`; a link\n277\t  there is left out.\n278\t- `ceo-restore.sh`:\n279\t  - checks every archive member before extraction (`_restore_archive.py`):\n280\t    no links, no absolute paths, no `..`, no name outside the layout, and\n281\t    never `audit-key` or `.salt`, in any letter case;\n282\t  - resolves `--dest` physically and refuses a link, or a file, as the\n283\t    destination;\n284\t  - refuses a restore target that is a link or sits under one;\n285\t  - runs the same checks in the preview and in the apply.\n286\t- On macOS, the system `tar` adds a hidden AppleDouble member\n287\t  (`._<name>`) for each file or folder with extended attributes, and\n288\t  `ceo-restore.sh` refused every such archive (`archive refused path:\n289\t  ._.`). `ceo-backup.sh` no longer writes these members. For archives\n290\t  that already have them, restore skips such a member, without writing\n291\t  it, only when its matching file or folder is in the same archive; any\n292\t  other `._` member is refused. Restore now extracts only the members it\n293\t  accepted, in Python instead of with `tar -x`: a truncated or corrupt\n294\t  archive is refused before anything is written, and so is an archive\n295\t  with a sparse member or with two members of the same name.\n296\t- The disaster-recovery and incident-response guides no longer pass\n297\t  `--dry-run`, a flag the script refuses. The preview is the default, and\n298\t  `--apply` restores.\n299\t\n300\t### Fixed — `audit-verify-chain.py --strict-against-counter`\n301\t\n302\t- With the flag, every result maps to a documented exit code with a named\n303\t  reason, one JSON object under `--json`, and no traceback.\n304\t- An absent counter exits 0 and reports `chain_length_source: \"absent\"`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"83b20cfd-a908-4fe3-8caa-faf4ef8d086d","uuid":"fa44e8b4-9880-478a-82f9-ec0f227e9583","timestamp":"2026-11-15T11:10:16.731Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.tG8bbW/tree/CHANGELOG.md","content":"\n- `scan()` in `_lib/secret_patterns.py` matched the NFKC-normalized view\n  of a text but reported offsets in that view, while `redact()` and\n  `scan_and_redact()` cut the original text with them. A character whose\n  NFKC form has another length (an ellipsis, a ligature) before a secret\n  shifted the cut, so part or all of the secret could survive next to its\n  label. The Codex and Grok egress redactor (`_lib/codex_egress_redact.py`)\n  uses these functions. Every release since v1.0.0 has the defect. The\n  signed v1.4.0 verdict declared this defect as its condition 58.\n- `scan()` now reports offsets in the text passed in. It matches that text\n  and its NFKC view, maps the view's matches back, and merges overlapping\n  findings into one span. The output is the original text with only those\n  spans replaced; no normalized character reaches it.\n- The pattern catalog is unchanged.\n- The fix is a commit the maintainer signed, after review by Codex and two\n  Claude refuters over several rounds.\n  Codex approved it with one condition, which predates the fix and is\n  declared in Known-open. No sentinel file covers it.\n\n### Fixed — backup and restore\n\n- `ceo-backup.sh` archives only the regular files of `memory/`; a link\n  there is left out.\n- `ceo-restore.sh`:\n  - checks every archive member before extraction (`_restore_archive.py`):\n    no links, no absolute paths, no `..`, no name outside the layout, and\n    never `audit-key` or `.salt`, in any letter case;\n  - resolves `--dest` physically and refuses a link, or a file, as the\n    destination;\n  - refuses a restore target that is a link or sits under one;\n  - runs the same checks in the preview and in the apply.\n- On macOS, the system `tar` adds a hidden AppleDouble member\n  (`._<name>`) for each file or folder with extended attributes, and\n  `ceo-restore.sh` refused every such archive (`archive refused path:\n  ._.`). `ceo-backup.sh` no longer writes these members. For archives\n  that already have them, restore skips such a member, without writing\n  it, only when its matching file or folder is in the same archive; any\n  other `._` member is refused. Restore now extracts only the members it\n  accepted, in Python instead of with `tar -x`: a truncated or corrupt\n  archive is refused before anything is written, and so is an archive\n  with a sparse member or with two members of the same name.\n- The disaster-recovery and incident-response guides no longer pass\n  `--dry-run`, a flag the script refuses. The preview is the default, and\n  `--apply` restores.\n\n### Fixed — `audit-verify-chain.py --strict-against-counter`\n\n- With the flag, every result maps to a documented exit code with a named\n  reason, one JSON object under `--json`, and no traceback.\n- An absent counter exits 0 and reports `chain_length_source: \"absent\"`.","numLines":50,"startLine":255,"totalLines":2658}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SM4WvaHwyqSPskHMtZ7XL4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
