SCOPE: payload 3/32. It has 12 new files under .claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_emit_parts/ (orchestration, pair_rail, planning, rate_limits, replay, resource_events, routing, security, streaming, tokens_boot, workflow, writer) and 3 under .claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_log_parts/ (__init__, adapters_runtime, entries_usage). They are PLAN-197 moves behind the _lib/audit_emit.py and audit_log.py facades.

WHAT I VERIFIED AGAINST THE CANDIDATE TREE
1. How the moved code is bound. audit_emit.py:413-452 calls _audit_emit_parts.install (__init__.py:20-48). For each part it runs import_module, copies the names in the part's __all__, deep-copies the values, and rebuilds every function with FunctionType(code, facade_globals). So in the facade:
   - global lookups resolve as before;
   - `global _FALLBACK_NOTIFIED` (writer.py:56-59) still works;
   - `globals().get("_plan088_rate_admit")` in _try_rate_admit (orchestration.py:288-296) still works;
   - patches of audit_emit.* still take effect.
   audit_log.py:311-327 runs the four part sources with exec in its own globals, in the order paths_skills, entries_usage, rotation_storage, adapters_runtime. The trailing `if __name__ == "__main__": sys.exit(main())` in adapters_runtime.py runs only after all four are installed.
2. Names. In each file of this payload, every name listed in __all__ is defined. Every free name these parts use at call time is defined in the facade or exported by some part's __all__. Examples:
   - _ATLAS_REGISTRY comes from adapters.py;
   - _PAIR_RAIL_REVIEW_ID_RE, _coerce_pair_rail_timeout_ms and _AUDIT_TOKENS_ALLOWLIST come from lifecycle_schemas.py;
   - _LEDGER_GATE_SIGNAL comes from harness_schemas.py;
   - _AUDIT_PRODUCER_PATH_POLLUTION_* comes from operations.py;
   - the estimate-drift and boot-task allowlists come from governance.py;
   - the boot and model-routing allowlists come from boot_schemas.py;
   - _PLAN088_RATE_LOCK and _PLAN090_BUCKET_LOCK are defined in the facade at audit_emit.py:483-484. They are used only at call time.
   None of the names I checked is defined twice, so install order cannot silently replace one.
3. Import-time safety and Python 3.9. Module-level code is limited to constants, frozensets and def statements. Default values evaluated at def time only use names the part itself defines or imports (time.time, time.monotonic, _PLAN088_PAYLOAD_MAX_BYTES). Every file has `from __future__ import annotations`, so module-level annotations such as orchestration's `_PLAN090_STREAM_BUCKETS: Dict[str, Tuple[float, int]]` are never evaluated. I found no syntax newer than 3.9 and nothing outside the standard library.
4. Fail direction.
   - _write_event, _breadcrumb and _write_fallback stay fail-open.
   - The chain-reset marker stays fail-closed for the chain and fail-open for the session.
   - The codex, grok and claude paths in adapters_runtime.py return 0 on every path. Their logic matches the staged copy .claude/plans/PLAN-182/staged-w1/tree/.claude/hooks/audit_log.py:1360-1615.
   - The PLAN-118 canonical-resolution check (audit_hmac.py:760-805) still keys on _lib.audit_emit, which stays in _lib/. The parts are imported from the same package.
   I found no inversion.
5. Kernel coverage. check_arbitration_kernel.py:312-313 lists _audit_emit_parts/**/* and _audit_log_parts/**/*. The segment glob treats ** as zero or more segments, so it also covers files directly in those folders. This is consistent with conditions 5 and 7.
6. Model value. In entries_usage.py, _ADR_052_ROLE_TO_MODEL maps "general-purpose" to claude-opus-5-5. This is only a fallback value for the audit log, not a routing switch. It arrived in v1.4.2 (CHANGELOG [1.4.2], PLAN-193), not in this delta. No model or effort setting changes here.

LIMITS: The tree has no copy of the base monolithic audit_emit.py, so I could not confirm that the moved text is identical to v1.4.3-rc.1. The redactor replaced the two literals at writer.py:423-424 with "[REDACTED:rg]", so I could not judge them. I had no shell, so nothing was executed.

FINDINGS: no P0, and no declared condition is false against this payload.

NEW FINDINGS (annex): no undeclared P1.

P2 FOLLOW-UPS
P2-1 — .claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_emit_parts/writer.py:603-604, 687, 758-761.
- Defect: the fallback branch reads `line` before it exists.
- Scenario: the audit directory or the lock directory exists but is not writable.
  1. FileLock calls os.open (filelock.py:144), which raises PermissionError before line 687 builds `line`.
  2. The `except (OSError, PermissionError)` branch calls `_write_fallback(line)`, which raises UnboundLocalError.
  3. The outer `except Exception` calls _breadcrumb, which writes into the same unwritable directory and fails silently.
  4. The event is lost, with no /tmp fallback and no banner. This contradicts the F-CHAOS-1 comment.
- Reach: only the sync path, meaning the spool kill-switch is on or the spool silently dropped the event.
- Test gap: test_audit_emit.py:1183 only fails at log.open, after `line` exists.
- Origin: the code looks carried over unchanged; the M9 comment at lines 741-748 already notes the same scoping problem for the timeout branch.
- Minimal fix: in the OSError branch, serialize from `event` the same way the FileLockTimeout branch does.

P2-2 — writer.py:42-51, 76, 87.
- Defect: the fallback file /tmp/ceo-audit-fallback-<user>.log has a predictable name, and both open("a") and os.chmod follow symlinks.
- Scenario: on a shared host (macOS has no protected_symlinks), another user plants a symlink at that path. The victim's degraded writes then append event JSON to any file the victim can write, and set that file to mode 0600.
- Origin: carried over by the move.
- Minimal fix: open with os.open(O_WRONLY|O_APPEND|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW, 0o600) and use fchmod on the descriptor.

P2-3 — .claude/hooks/audit_log.py:317-327 (the loader that runs this payload's _audit_log_parts files).
- Defect: a missing or unreadable part file raises FileNotFoundError at import. The hook exits 1 with a traceback.
- Mismatch: its own docstring ("Safety properties 1") promises a breadcrumb and exit 0, and the same file keeps a partial-upgrade fallback for runtime_paths at lines 180-199.
- Impact: exit 1 on PostToolUse does not block the session, so this is not a fail-direction inversion. The audit row is lost and the user sees noise.
- Minimal fix: wrap _install_parts in try/except, write a breadcrumb, and exit 0 when run as __main__.

P2-4 — doc drift caused by the move.
- SPEC/v1/soc2-control-map.schema.md:524: the CC7.1 verification method `grep '^def emit_' .claude/hooks/_lib/audit_emit.py` now finds only emit_credential_rotation_due and emit_generic. The typed emitters now live in _audit_emit_parts/*.py.
- Stale code pointers: pair_rail.py:85-86 and routing.py:420 cite "audit_emit.py:386-396" and "grep -F assertions"; orchestration.py:217 cites "line ~3484".
- Minimal fix: point the verification method at both the facade and the parts, and update the pointers.

P2-5 — name lookup in the facade for annotations.
- Defect: these parts' string annotations use names the facade does not import (audit_emit.py:12 imports only Any, Dict, Optional):
  - List: routing.py, tokens_boot.py, workflow.py;
  - Tuple: orchestration.py, rate_limits.py, tokens_boot.py;
  - Callable: rate_limits.py, streaming.py;
  - Iterator: replay.py.
  As a result, typing.get_type_hints() on those bound emitters raises NameError.
- Impact: no in-scope code calls get_type_hints. test_audit_emit.py:1567 imports it but introspects with inspect.signature.
- Related hygiene: _audit_log_parts/entries_usage.py and adapters_runtime.py cannot be imported as standalone modules (re, os and sys are unbound), even though the directory is a package. In production they are only run by the facade.
- Minimal fix: import the missing typing names in the facade, and document that _audit_log_parts is exec-only.

VERDICT: GO These moved audit_emit and audit_log parts bind every name they use into the facade, stay standard-library-only and Python 3.9 compatible, keep their fail-open behavior and are on the kernel list, so no declared condition is contradicted and no P0 was found; what remains are P2 follow-ups, mostly carried over from before the split.
Payload 4: no P0 or falsified declared condition established. Namespace, path-resolution and 30 in-memory CLI smoke checks passed on Python 3.9.6; label persistence was mocked. The findings below are newly observed, not proven regressions introduced by extraction.

NEW FINDINGS (annex)
P1 — .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/fact_state.py:327
The default-enabled shadow gate persists state using a predictable temporary pathname opened with O_TRUNC. A pre-existing symlink there redirects truncation and JSON writes into another adopter file; a symlinked parent also redirects storage. The subsequent replace does not undo that damage. Minimal fix: reject symlinked parents, create a unique temporary file exclusively inside a verified directory, and replace through that directory; skip persistence when storage is unsafe. Confirmed by source tracing, without exercising destructive writes.

P2 follow-ups
P2 — .claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_query/domain.py:251
Malformed sunset-policy boolean values become False, contradicting the documented True defaults for unparseable values at lines 214–215. This disables hint and UNKNOWN filtering and can report spurious reopening candidates. Both false results were confirmed through the CLI facade. Minimal fix: recognize explicit true/false values and retain True for invalid or empty values.

VERDICT: GO-WITH-CONDITIONS The identified unsafe-write defect and reporting follow-up belong in the annex under this cut’s rule; neither establishes a P0 or disproves a declared condition.

